# 🦑 Multiplayer

Lets a squid swim out of its tank and into somebody else's, over the LAN. The
two squid can perceive each other, act on what they perceive, and remember the
individual afterwards.

**Experimental.** LAN only — no internet transport, no matchmaking, no NAT
traversal. Not enabled at startup (`auto_load_blacklist` in `plugin_manager.py`);
turn it on from the Plugin manager.

## Rules and risks

> Multiplayer is optional. Enabling it exposes the squid to new experiences
> but may ultimately result in losing its life. The user takes this risk.

That notice is shown the first time multiplayer is enabled (remembered as
`[Multiplayer] risk_notice_shown` in `config.ini`). It is the whole bargain.

**Whether to mollycoddle your squid or foster its independence is up to you.**
Keep multiplayer off and it never meets another squid: it cannot fight, it
cannot mate, and no egg is ever laid - reproduction has no other way to
happen. Turn multiplayer on and it can swim off into other tanks, where its own
brain - not you, and not the other player - decides what it does with whoever
it meets.

What it can find out there:

- **Other squid.** A squid arriving in the tank catches the resident's eye.
  Meeting others grows lasting structure in the brain - senses for other squid
  and social neurons for playing with, fighting or fleeing from them - and each
  individual it meets, if the meeting mattered, is remembered as that
  individual.
- **Contests and fights.** Two squid going for the same object contest it;
  close by, that can become a fight (one cloud over both) with a winner and a
  loser, counted in each squid's statistics and towards the Fisticuffs!,
  Scuffle, Tussle and Slugfest achievements. Personality matters: a timid
  squid never starts a fight, though it can be drawn into one.
- **A mate.** Two squid that stay in contact for 2 seconds during a visit get
  one 2% roll for that visit (see [Reproduction](#reproduction)). Only the
  host tank's resident is affected: it lays an egg and becomes a parent.

The risk, in full:

1. **A parent stops eating.** It will not touch food again, stays home with its
   egg, grows hungry on the ordinary metabolism and, once starving, loses
   health until it dies. Starting from a fed squid that takes roughly 20-30
   minutes of play at normal speed; sleep pauses it, medicine only delays it.
2. **When it dies, its egg hatches** into a new squid with a newborn brain. The
   parent's save, memories and learning are gone; its achievements carry on,
   CIRCLE OF LIFE among them.

Neither risk can be undone once the roll has gone against you - which is why
the choice is made before the first visit, not after.

### Any squid can die

These have nothing to do with multiplayer and apply to every squid, plugin on
or off:

| Cause | When |
|---|---|
| Neglect | hunger stays at 100 (the hungriest) for 5 minutes of play; any meal resets it |
| Sickness | it stays sick for 10 minutes of play without medicine; medicine resets it |
| Starvation | a parent, as above |

Every dead squid becomes a **headstone** - one of ten, at random - on the tank
floor where it died. Click it to read "RIP *name*". The squid can see it and
push it like any decoration; it can never be picked up, thrown or deleted, and
it is never bigger than 80% of a squid. Headstones stay through hatches and New
Games. After a death, the parent's egg hatches - or, with no egg, a new squid
hatches from a fresh one.

| Setting (`config.ini` `[Lifecycle]`) | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `mating_chance` | 0.02 | chance of the one roll a visit gets |
| `mating_contact_seconds` | 2.0 | sustained contact before that roll |
| `starvation_hunger` | 90 | hunger at which a parent starts losing health |
| `neglect_hunger` | 100 | hunger counted as neglect |
| `neglect_seconds` | 300 | seconds of play at that hunger before death |
| `sickness_seconds` | 600 | seconds of play sick, untreated, before death |

## The invariant

> A visiting squid's brain stays on its own machine. The host runs its body,
> not its mind.

```
        HOST TANK                              VISITOR'S HOME INSTANCE
        ---------                              -----------------------
  what the visitor can see  --perception_frame->  its own sensors
                                                  its own propagation
                                                  its own action neurons
  apply it, resolve it      <--action_intent----  decision_engine.select_action
  what actually happened    --consequence------>  its own senses again
                                                  its own memory
```

The action selection is `src/decision_engine.select_action` — the same function
a squid uses at home, not a copy. A second copy would be a second squid, and the
two would drift.

## What crosses the wire

Two rules decide what may be in a message: **only what the other squid could
actually observe**, and **private cognitive state never leaves home**.

| Direction | Message | Carries |
|-----------|---------|---------|
| host → visitor | `perception_frame` | position, tank bounds, facing, five observable sensor values (`can_see_food`, `plant_proximity`, `external_stimulus`, `squid_in_view`, `squid_proximity`), the resident's identity, host-issued item ids |
| visitor → host | `action_intent` | an action **neuron name**, its activation, confidence, heading, target id, lease |
| host → visitor | `consequence` | what happened: a contest resolved, food eaten, an action blocked, a mating (its id only) |
| either | `visit_request` / `visit_response` / `visit_end` | consent, and the end of a visit |

**Never crosses:** weights, neuron activations, drives, memories, the capability
ledger, neurogenesis state, scene contents, or any filesystem path. A message
containing any of them is rejected on arrival at any nesting depth
(`FORBIDDEN_KEYS` in `remote_protocol.py`).

A frame carries only those five; any other sensor name a host sends is dropped
on arrival. There is nothing of the visitor's a host could assert: no neuron
stands for another squid, and its memories never leave home.

### An intent is a lease, not a command

Every `action_intent` expires. If the network drops after the visitor asks to
swim left, the host stops applying it when the lease runs out and the visitor
**stops** — it does not carry on, and it does not get a stand-in policy to keep
it looking alive. Both would be the host inventing behaviour.

## Encounters

**No neuron is added for another squid.** A squid sees another squid the way it
sees food: with its own vision (`src/vision_worker.py`). A visitor is drawn in
the tank as category `squid`, and the squid's view cone (72°, shared by desktop,
host and mobile in `src/vision_cone.py`) decides whether it is in view. That
reaches the brain as one **percept**, `squid_in_view` — a value the world sets,
not a neuron.

- **Arrival catches the eye.** When the host admits a visitor, the resident's
  view cone swings onto it and follows it for a few seconds
  (`Squid.notice`) — the orienting response — then scanning resumes. Whether
  it is seen is still vision's call. Asleep, it notices nothing. On mobile,
  where a squid sees the way it faces, the resident turns to face it.
- **Nothing for other squid is innate, and nothing is chosen for the squid.**
  Neurogenesis grows what it needs the first time it matters, each with its
  cause on record:
  - **sense organs**, written by the world like `can_see_food`:
    `can_see_squid` (a tick box) on the first sighting, `squid_nearby` the first
    time one comes close, `recognises_squid` (a tick box) the first time it
    meets one it remembers, and `remembers_bad_encounter` /
    `remembers_good_encounter` the first time that memory is good or bad;
  - **social neurons**, hexagons named for their purpose, the first time the
    squid's own network does the thing with another squid in view:
    `social_play` (its play neuron fired), `social_flee` (its flight neuron
    fired) or `social_fight` (it was in a fight). Each is fed by the senses
    and drives the squid's existing actions; learning moves every synapse.
- **Personality is wiring.** It enters only as how `can_see_squid` is wired
  when it grows — towards the other squid for most, away for a timid one —
  so what a squid first does on seeing another is its network's response.
- **Recognition is memory.** What a squid remembers of the individual in its
  view cone comes from its own long-term memory (`PeerLedger`), never from
  the other squid or its host. Encounters that mattered (fights, thefts,
  matings, ones that moved its drives) are kept; mild ones are forgotten.
- **`act_play` at a squid in view** swims at it, if it is nearer than any rock;
  face to face, that is a contest, which can become a fight. A visitor's play
  intent does the same on the host. Nothing here maps "another squid" to an
  action: the grown neurons drive the squid's own action neurons, and the
  decision engine reads the winner.

Saves from earlier versions had nine `conspecific_*` sensor neurons. They are
dropped, with their synapses, as such a save loads
(`brain_constants.RETIRED_NEURONS`).

`encounter_sensors.py` keeps the tank's record of who is present and what passed
between them (positions, contests), which encounters, memories, fights and the
fight cloud are built from. It registers no neuron.

### Memory

A visit becomes an ordinary memory, filed against the individual:

```
category 'social', key 'peer:<squid uuid>'
```

Identity is `Squid.uuid`, which the save file already persists and restores —
not `node_id`, which is regenerated every session and so cannot answer "is this
the same squid I met last week".

`PeerLedger` is a **view** over the squid's own `MemoryManager`, not a second
store. Long-term memory already persists, already promotes what matters, and
already raises the importance of a repeated experience; a parallel database
would drift from it. What gets recorded is objective — an object changed hands,
food was eaten, how the squid's drives moved — never a verdict about the peer.

## Reproduction

Reproduction happens only here, during a visit, and only the **host** decides
it - the same way it resolves a contest. There is no mating action, here or in
either brain: the visitor's own decisions move its body, the resident's move
its own, and the host works out what that means in its tank.

1. If the visitor's body stays within reach of the resident
   (`MATING_CONTACT_RANGE`) for `mating_contact_seconds`, the host makes **one
   roll for the whole visit** at `mating_chance` (2% by default). Brief
   contact never rolls, so reproduction is rare and never routine.
2. Only a resident that is at home, alive and has never reproduced can mate.
   The visitor's state is not consulted - the host does not have it.
3. On success the host records it in the **core** lifecycle
   (`src/lifecycle.py`, via `TamagotchiLogic.record_reproduction`): the
   resident becomes a parent and its egg is laid in its own tank, as **one
   record in one atomic save**. Only then is the visitor told.
4. The visitor is sent `consequence` `mated` with the mating id
   (`<visit id>-mate`) and nothing else, on three round trips in a row (UDP),
   and again whenever that same individual visits later. It files the mating
   once, by id, as an ordinary encounter memory
   (`peer:<uuid>:mating:<id>`) - its body and lifecycle are untouched.

What follows is not a multiplayer concern and keeps happening with the plugin
off: the parent stops eating, starves on the ordinary metabolism (or dies
sooner of a sickness left untreated), dies once, becomes a headstone, and its
egg hatches into a new game (see `src/lifecycle.py` and [Rules and
risks](#rules-and-risks)). A dead resident ends every visit, its own included
(`on_squid_died`), and refuses new ones.

## The fight cloud

When a contest breaks out between two squid that are both in the tank and
within `FIGHT_CLOUD_RANGE` of each other, **one** animated cloud
(`images/fight_cloud.gif`) is drawn between them for a couple of seconds. It
is a drawing and nothing else. If the GIF is not installed, nothing is drawn.

When the host records a mating, **one** beating pixel heart
(`images/mating_heart.gif`) is drawn over the pair the same way, for about
three seconds. It is shown only once the mating is recorded and saved, and like
the cloud it is a drawing and nothing else.

## Consent

Entry is something the host agrees to. A refused visitor gets no body and no
autopilot, so it cannot enter.

| Mode | Behaviour |
|------|-----------|
| `open` | every visitor admitted (the default; fine on a trusted LAN) |
| `known` | only peers on the allow list |
| `closed` | nobody |

## Timings

| Constant | Default | Meaning |
|----------|---------|---------|
| `ROUND_TRIP_INTERVAL` | 0.5 s | one perception → decision → action cycle |
| `INTENT_LEASE` | 1.5 s | how long an intent stays valid |
| `LINK_TIMEOUT` | 4.0 s | silence after which a visitor stops / a visitor comes home |
| `VISIT_ABANDON_TIMEOUT` | 12.0 s | silence after which the host ends the visit |
| `SYNC_INTERVAL` | 1.0 s | state broadcast cadence |
| `MULTICAST_GROUP` : `PORT` | `224.3.29.71:10000` | where peers find each other |

## Files

**The round trip**

| File | What it owns |
|------|--------------|
| `remote_protocol.py` | the four messages, their validation, and what may not cross |
| `host_body.py` | the host as a visitor's body: perception out, intents in, consequences back |
| `visitor_mind.py` | the visitor's end — its own brain, its own senses, its own memory |

**Encounters**

| File | What it owns |
|------|--------------|
| `encounter_sensors.py` | who is present in the tank and what passed between them (no neurons) |
| `encounter.py` | an encounter as a bout with an outcome, measured from drives |
| `peer_ledger.py` | what this squid remembers about individuals, over `MemoryManager` |
| `identity.py` | `SquidIdentity` — persistent, wire-safe |
| `consent.py` | who may enter |

**Transport and UI**

| File | What it owns |
|------|--------------|
| `main.py` | plugin entry point: registers `MultiplayerPlugin` with the plugin manager |
| `mp_plugin_logic.py` | the plugin itself: lifecycle, timers, message handlers, wiring |
| `mp_network_node.py` | the multicast socket, the listener thread, peer discovery |
| `mp_constants.py` | group, port, cadence, plugin metadata |
| `network_utilities.py` | message compression and node-id helpers |
| `packet_validator.py` | packet-level checks before anything is parsed |
| `remote_entity_manager.py` | drawing remote squid |
| `asset_paths.py` | turning a remote item name into a local asset, safely |
| `multiplayer_status_widget.py`, `status_bar_component.py`, `multiplayer_config_dialog.py` | UI |
| `__init__.py` | empty; makes the folder a package |

**Dead code**

Two files are no longer reachable. Both are kept for reference and should be
deleted once nothing wants to look at them.

| File | Why it is dead |
|------|----------------|
| `squid_multiplayer_autopilot.py` | `RemoteSquidController` is the stand-in brain the round trip replaced. Nothing appends to `pending_controller_creations`, so `_setup_controller_immediately` never runs and no controller is ever constructed. **While it exists, re-wiring it would silently reintroduce a second brain on the host** — the one thing the design forbids. |
| `multiplayer_events.py` | `MultiplayerEventDispatcher` is imported by nothing. Message dispatch goes through the plugin manager's hooks (`on_network_*`) instead. |

## Running two instances

Start the game twice on one machine (or on two machines on the same LAN), enable
Multiplayer in both, and open **Plugins → Multiplayer → Network Dashboard**.

Each should list the other under **Detected Peers** within a second or two.

**If Detected Peers stays empty**, read the dashboard's stats:

| Reading | Means |
|---------|-------|
| Listener: **NOT RUNNING** | the receive thread did not start — nothing can ever be detected |
| Sent climbing, Received **0** | this instance is talking and not hearing. Multicast is not reaching it: check the firewall (on Windows, inbound UDP for `python.exe`), and that both are on the same subnet |
| Both climbing, no peers | packets arrive but are rejected — run with `multiplayer_debug = True` in `config.ini` |

Sending and listening are independent, which is worth remembering: an instance
can log *"Exit message successfully broadcast"* every time while hearing nothing
at all.

## Tests

| File | Covers |
|------|--------|
| `tests/test_peer_discovery.py` | real sockets: two nodes on one machine must hear each other |
| `tests/test_social_concept.py` | vision sees other squid, arrival catches the eye, the grown social neurons, old saves |
| `tests/test_encounter_stage0.py` | identity, encounter memory, safe asset paths, consent |
| `tests/test_remote_mind_stage1.py` | the round trip, and that the plugin does not choose the visitor's behaviour |
| `tests/test_social_consequences_stage2a.py` | what a squid keeps of an encounter, filed against that individual only |
| `tests/test_reproduction_lifecycle.py` | mating authority, rarity, one egg, retransmission and disconnects, starvation, save/load, the fight cloud |
| `tests/test_new_game_and_hatch.py` | New Game and the hatch, through the real main window |

The load-bearing one is `PluginDoesNotChooseBehaviourTests` in the Stage 1 file.
Every other test could pass while this plugin quietly decided what a visitor did;
that one fails if it does.

## Not built yet

Signalling, gossip, asynchronous visits to offline tanks, internet transport,
and any social behaviour beyond individual-specific memory and the
host-resolved consequences above.
